  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nLong and medium range models continue to show a heavy rain and \nsignificant flash flooding event Tuesday night through Thursday of \nthis week. A strengthening low pressure system moving east across \nNew Mexico and into western and northern Texas may combine with the \nremnants of Hurricane Polo. Both of these systems combined with a \nfrontal boundary across our area will provide more than enough \nsupport for widespread rain and thunderstorm activity. Abundant \nmoisture to include precipitable water values in the 1.9\" to 2.5\" \nrange will support efficient rain production with this activity. \nModel depictions continue to show multiple rounds of precipitation \nto include some degree of thunderstorm training occurring. The \nbiggest source of uncertainty is where exactly the bands of heavy \nrainfall will setup at and how quickly they will be moving. With \nrespect to rainfall totals, widespread rainfall totals of 2 to 4 \ninches are depicted along with locally heavier pockets of 7+ \ninches. This type of activity will pose a Moderate Risk (40-70% \nchance) of excessive rainfall leading to flash flooding for \nTuesday night through Thursday. As a result, a Flood Watch has \nbeen issued from late tonight through late Thursday night.\n\nAdditionally, the Storm Prediction Center has outlooked a good \nportion of our area in a Slight Risk for seeing severe weather on \nWednesday afternoon into Thursday as well. Damaging winds and large \nhail are the main concerns, but an isolated tornado cannot be ruled \nout completely.",
